module lib/minpack/lmder  documents every variable
  Nonlinear least squares: find the parameters $x$ (n numbers) that make m
  residuals $f_1(x), \ldots, f_m(x)$ as small as possible, in the sense of the
  sum of their squares $\lVert f(x) \rVert^2 = \sum_i f_i(x)^2$. Fitting a model
  to data is the usual case: $x$ holds the model's parameters, and $f_i$ is the
  model's prediction at the i-th data point minus the measured value. Squaring
  makes every residual count whatever its sign, and makes the sum smooth, so
  derivatives can guide the search.

  This is a port of MINPACK's lmder (Moré, Garbow and Hillstrom, Argonne,
  1980), the routine behind scipy.optimize.leastsq and many other fitting
  codes. It needs the Jacobian $J$, the m x n matrix of derivatives
  $J_{ij} = \partial f_i / \partial x_j$: row i says how residual i changes when
  each parameter moves. Near $x$, $f$ is close to its linear model
  $f(x + p) \approx f(x) + J p$, and minimizing $\lVert f + J p \rVert^2$ over the
  step $p$ is a linear least-squares problem, whose answer is the Gauss-Newton
  step. The model is only good near $x$, so lmder limits the step to a trust
  region $\lVert D p \rVert \le \Delta$: a ball of radius $\Delta$ (an
  ellipsoid, with the scaling $D$) inside which the model is trusted.

  Limiting the step's length is the same as adding a penalty
  $\lambda \lVert D p \rVert^2$ to the model's sum of squares; the $\lambda$ that
  makes the step exactly as long as $\Delta$ is the Levenberg-Marquardt
  parameter, and lmpar finds it. After each trial step lmder compares the
  reduction of the sum of squares it got with the reduction the linear model
  predicted, and grows or shrinks $\Delta$ by how well they agree.

  Two loops. The outer one computes one Jacobian per pass and factors it. The
  inner one proposes trial steps from that factorization until one of them
  lowers the sum of squares, shrinking $\Delta$ after each failure; most passes
  need one trial.

  The Fortran is one routine of about 150 statements held together by go-to
  statements; the steps below name its blocks, in their original order.
  lib/minpack/README.md maps each step to the Fortran's labels.

lmder  @b769c32b
  what             Minimize $\lVert f(x) \rVert^2$ over $x$, starting from the $x$ passed
                   in, with the Jacobian supplied by fcn. Returns info, the reason it
                   stopped; x and fvec hold the answer and its residuals, the top n x n
                   of fjac holds R of the last QR factorization, ipvt its column order,
                   and qtf the first n entries of $Q^\top f$.
  how              Outer loop: evaluate $J$, factor it as $J P = Q R$ (qrfac), measure
                   how nearly $f$ is orthogonal to the columns of $J$ (gnorm), and
                   update the scaling $D$. Inner loop: ask lmpar for the $\lambda$ and
                   step $p$ with $\lVert D p \rVert$ close to $\Delta$, evaluate $f$ at
                   $x + p$, and compare the actual reduction of $\lVert f \rVert^2$
                   with the model's prediction. Their ratio $\rho$ decides everything
                   that follows: at most $\tfrac14$ (`LM_RATIO_SHRINK`) the radius
                   shrinks, at least $\tfrac34$ (`LM_RATIO_EXPAND`) it doubles, and the
                   step is taken if $\rho$ is at least $10^{-4}$ (`LM_RATIO_ACCEPT`).
                   The loops end when one of the tolerance tests passes.
  why              A plain Gauss-Newton step can be far too long where $f$ is strongly
                   curved. On Rosenbrock's function from the standard start, the first
                   trial is the Gauss-Newton step, 71.7 long in a radius of 3051; the
                   sum of squares goes up instead of down (the norm from 4.92 to 48.4),
                   the ratio is -95.8, and the radius drops to 7.17. The next trial,
                   with $\lambda = 0.0078$, is accepted
                   [test "Rosenbrock's first trial is the Gauss-Newton step, 71.7 long in a radius of 3051; f grows from 4.92 to 48.4, ratio -95.8, and the radius drops to 7.17"].
                   On that problem 5 of the 20 trial steps are rejected and the norm
                   never rises [test "5 of the 20 trial steps on Rosenbrock are rejected, and fnorm never rises"],
                   and every recorded number of every trial step equals the Fortran's
                   [test "all 20 trial steps on Rosenbrock equal the Fortran's, every recorded number to the last bit"].
  fcn              the user's def: residuals into fvec for iflag 1, the Jacobian into
                   fjac for iflag 2; returns iflag, or a negative number to stop
  x                $x$  the parameters: the starting point on entry, the answer on
                   return; a trial point is formed apart from it, in wa2
  fvec             $f$  the m residuals at the current $x$
  fjac             $J$  the m x n Jacobian at the current $x$; after the qr step its
                   upper triangle holds $R$ and the part below holds the Householder
                   vectors that define $Q$
  fnorm            $\lVert f \rVert$  the Euclidean norm of the residuals at the
                   current $x$
  fnorm1           $\lVert f_{+} \rVert$  the norm of the residuals at the trial point
                   $x + p$
  delta            $\Delta$  the trust radius: steps must satisfy
                   $\lVert D p \rVert \le \Delta$ (lmpar aims for $\lVert D p \rVert$
                   within a tenth of $\Delta$ when the Gauss-Newton step is longer)
  delta_in         $\Delta_{\mathrm{in}}$  the radius lmpar was given for this trial,
                   kept for hist
  diag             $D$  the scaling, one positive number per variable. By default it is
                   the norm of each column of the first Jacobian, and never decreases
                   after. It measures each variable by how much it moves $f$, so
                   rescaling a variable does not change the iterates.
  par              $\lambda$  the Levenberg-Marquardt parameter: the step solves
                   $(J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D)\, p = -J^\top f$. Zero gives the
                   Gauss-Newton step; a large $\lambda$ gives a short step close to
                   steepest descent. lmder carries it from one call of lmpar to the
                   next as a starting guess.
  par_lm           $\lambda_{\mathrm{lm}}$  the $\lambda$ lmpar returned for this trial,
                   before the radius update changes par
  pnorm            $\lVert D p \rVert$  the scaled length of the trial step
  actred           $\mathrm{ared}$  the actual relative reduction of the sum of
                   squares, $1 - \lVert f_{+} \rVert^2 / \lVert f \rVert^2$
  prered           $\mathrm{pred}$  the reduction the linear model predicts, in the
                   same units
  ratio            $\rho$  $\mathrm{ared} / \mathrm{pred}$: 1 means the model was exact, a
                   negative value means the sum of squares went up
  temp1            $t_1$  $\lVert J p \rVert / \lVert f \rVert$
  temp2            $t_2$  $\sqrt{\lambda}\, \lVert D p \rVert / \lVert f \rVert$
  gnorm            $\gamma$  the largest cosine of the angle between $f$ and a column
                   of $J$; zero at a stationary point, where $J^\top f = 0$
  xnorm            $\lVert D x \rVert$  the scaled size of $x$, which the xtol test
                   measures $\Delta$ against
  qtf              $Q^\top f$  the first n entries of the residuals rotated by $Q^\top$
  iter             $k$  the outer iteration: 1 plus the number of steps taken so far
  epsmch           $\varepsilon$  the machine precision, as MINPACK's dpmpar gives it
  LM_RATIO_SHRINK  $\tfrac14$  at most this $\rho$ and the radius shrinks
  LM_RATIO_EXPAND  $\tfrac34$  at least this $\rho$ and the radius doubles
  LM_RATIO_ACCEPT  $10^{-4}$  at least this $\rho$ and the step is taken
  LM_SHRINK_MIN    $0.1$  the smallest factor a shrink multiplies the radius by
  LM_RISE_LIMIT    $0.1$  a trial norm at least ten times the current one counts as
                   $\mathrm{ared} = -1$
  hist             one row per trial step, in the columns H_ITER ... H_X; see the
                   header of lmder.viv
  data             the array lmder hands to fcn unchanged: whatever the problem needs
                   (here its number in MINPACK's test set and its sizes)
  ftol             stop (info 1) when both the actual and the predicted relative
                   reduction of the sum of squares are at most this
  xtol             stop (info 2) when the trust radius is at most this fraction of
                   $\lVert D x \rVert$: $x$ is then known to about this relative accuracy
  gtol             stop (info 4) when gnorm is at most this; lmder1 passes 0, so the
                   test fires only at an exact stationary point
  maxfev           the most evaluations of f allowed; reaching it stops lmder (info 5)
  mode             how the scaling $D$ is chosen: 1, from the Jacobian's column norms
                   (lmder1's choice); 2, the caller's diag, used as given
  factor           the first radius as a multiple of $\lVert D x_0 \rVert$
  nprint           call fcn with iflag 0 every nprint passes so it can print
                   progress; 0 or less never does
  ipvt             $\pi$  the column order of the last QR factorization: column j of
                   $J P$ is column $\pi_j$ of $J$
  counts           on return, the number of evaluations of f and of J
  m                $m$  the number of residuals
  n                $n$  the number of parameters
  wa1              a work vector of n numbers, allocated once; the Fortran reuses it
                   for different things, and the lines below say what it holds where
  wa1@qr           the diagonal of $R$, which qrfac returns here (fjac's own
                   diagonal holds the Householder vectors); the qtf step puts it back
                   into fjac
  wa1@lm_parameter $u$  the step lmpar returns, with its sign flipped: $u = -p$
  wa1@trial        $p$  the trial step, after the sign flip
  wa2              a work vector of n numbers, reused like wa1
  wa2@qr           $\lVert J_{:,j} \rVert$  the norm of each column of $J$, which
                   qrfac returns here; the scale, gradient and rescale steps read it
  wa2@lm_parameter scratch space for lmpar: the diagonal of the damped triangle it
                   builds (its sdiag)
  wa2@trial        $x + p$  the trial point
  wa2@accept#1     $D x$  the new point, scaled, for its norm xnorm
  wa3              a work vector of n numbers, reused like wa1
  wa3@qr           scratch space for qrfac (the column norms it last computed from
                   scratch)
  wa3@first_radius $D x_0$  the starting point, scaled, for its norm
  wa3@lm_parameter scratch space for lmpar
  wa3@trial        $D p$  the trial step, scaled, for its norm pnorm
  wa3@predicted    $R P^\top p$  the step pushed through the triangle: its norm is
                   $\lVert J p \rVert$, because $Q$ changes no lengths
  wa4              a work vector of m numbers, reused like wa1
  wa4@qtf          $Q^\top f$  being built: f with the Householder reflections
                   applied one at a time
  wa4@lm_parameter scratch space for lmpar
  wa4@evaluate     $f(x + p)$  the residuals at the trial point; accept copies them
                   into fvec
  info             why lmder stopped: 0 bad input, 1 to 3 converged, 4 the gradient
                   test, 5 out of evaluations, 6 to 8 a tolerance too small for
                   double precision, negative when fcn asked to stop
  iflag            what fcn returned last: the iflag it was given, or a negative
                   number when the user's function asks lmder to stop
  nfev             how many times f has been evaluated
  njev             how many times $J$ has been evaluated: one per pass
  nrow             the row of hist the next trial step goes into
  ok               whether the input passed every check
  quit             true once a test has set info or fcn asked to stop; it ends the
                   outer loop
  j                a column index, 0 to n - 1: one variable, or one column of $J$
                   or $R$
  i                a row index: one residual, or one row of $R$
  ii               how far down column j a loop has gone, from row j
  l                $\pi_j$  the column of $J$ in position j after pivoting (ipvt[j])
  sum              a dot product being added up
  sum@qtf          $u_j^\top w$  Householder vector j dotted with the residuals
                   rotated so far (wa4)
  sum@gradient     $(J^\top f)_{\pi_j} / \lVert f \rVert$  computed as row j of
                   $R^\top Q^\top f$, over $\lVert f \rVert$
  temp             a number held for the next line or two
  temp@qtf         $-u_j^\top w / u_{jj}$  how much of Householder vector j to add to
                   w: that is the reflection $w \gets w - u_j (u_j^\top w) / u_{jj}$
  temp@predicted   $p_{\pi_j}$  the step's component for the variable in position j
  temp@shrink      $t$  the factor the radius shrinks by: $\tfrac12$, or, when the sum
                   of squares went up, where the parabola through what was seen has
                   its minimum; never below $\tfrac1{10}$
  q                $\lVert f_{+} \rVert / \lVert f \rVert$  the trial norm over the
                   current one
  dirder           half the slope of $\lVert f(x + t p) \rVert^2 / \lVert f \rVert^2$ at
                   $t = 0$, from the model: $-(t_1^2 + t_2^2)$. The shrink step fits a
                   parabola to it

  setup          allocate the work vectors and start every counter at zero
  check_input    refuse n = 0, fewer residuals than variables, a negative tolerance, a nonpositive maxfev or factor, and (mode 2) a nonpositive scale
    why          MINPACK does not guess what a caller meant: bad input returns info 0 before anything is evaluated.
  if the input is valid
    start          evaluate f at the starting point and its norm fnorm
      why          Everything after compares the norm of the residuals with this one: a step is only worth taking if it lowers it.
      run          The program starts from $x$ = {x}, where $\lVert f \rVert$ = {fnorm}.
  repeat once per Jacobian, until a test sets info or fcn asks to stop
    jacobian       evaluate J at x (fcn with iflag 2)
      why          The Jacobian is what makes the linear model $f(x + p) \approx f + J p$: every step of this pass is computed from it, so it is evaluated once per pass, after a step was accepted.
      $$ J_{ij} = \frac{\partial f_i}{\partial x_j} $$
      run          On the first pass, at $x$ = {x}, the Jacobian is $J$ = {fjac}.
    qr             factor J P = Q R with column pivoting; the column norms of J come back in wa2
      why          Everything the rest of the pass needs is in $R$ and $Q^\top f$. Solving the step's least-squares problem from $R$ is also more accurate than forming $J^\top J$, which squares the condition number of $J$.
      how          $Q$ is orthogonal ($Q^\top Q = I$, so it changes no lengths), $R$ is upper triangular, and $P$ reorders the columns of $J$ so that at each stage the column with the most norm left comes first (column pivoting). qrfac, the next section, does the work.
      $$ J P = Q R $$
    if this is the first pass
      scale          mode 1: D = the column norms of J (a zero column gets 1), so each variable is measured by how much it moves f
        why          Without scaling, a variable measured in millimeters and one in kilometers would share one trust radius. With $D_j = \lVert J_{:,j} \rVert$, the length $\lVert D p \rVert$ counts each variable's change by its effect on $f$.
        $$ D_j = \lVert J_{:,j} \rVert $$
        run          On this problem $D$ = {diag}: the first variable moves $f$ more than twice as much as the second.
      first_radius   start the trust radius at factor * |D x| (factor * 1 if x = 0): generous, so the first trial is usually the Gauss-Newton step
        why          The first radius is a guess. lmder1 passes factor 100, so the first trial is almost always the Gauss-Newton step, and the radius update corrects the guess from what that step does.
        $$ \Delta_0 = \mathrm{factor} \cdot \lVert D x_0 \rVert $$
        predict      With $\Delta$ a hundred times the scaled size of $x$, will the first trial need $\lambda > 0$ to stay inside the region?
        run          $\lVert D x \rVert$ = {xnorm}, so $\Delta$ = {delta}. The Gauss-Newton step is far shorter than that, and lmpar returns $\lambda = 0$ on the first trial.
    qtf            apply the Householder reflections to f to get Q^T f, keep its first n entries in qtf, and put R's diagonal back on fjac's
      why          Because $Q$ changes no lengths, the model's sum of squares splits into a part the step can change and a part it cannot. Only the first n entries of $Q^\top f$ matter for the step.
      $$ \lVert f + J p \rVert^2 = \lVert (Q^\top f)_{1:n} + R P^\top p \rVert^2 + \lVert (Q^\top f)_{n+1:m} \rVert^2 $$
    gradient       gnorm = the largest |cos| of the angle between f and a column of J, from R^T (Q^T f) / |f| / column norm; info 4 if gnorm <= gtol
      why          At a minimum of $\lVert f \rVert^2$ its gradient $2 J^\top f$ is zero: $f$ is orthogonal to every column of $J$, and no small step can lower the sum. gnorm measures how close to that the current $x$ is, as a cosine, so it does not depend on the size of $f$.
      $$ \gamma = \max_j \frac{\left| (J^\top f)_j \right|}{\lVert J_{:,j} \rVert\, \lVert f \rVert} $$
      run          On the first pass $\gamma$ = {gnorm}: far from a stationary point.
    if D is chosen internally (mode 1)
      rescale        D = max(D, current column norms): scales only grow, so the region never widens behind the algorithm's back
    repeat with trial steps from this factorization, until one is accepted or a test stops it
      lm_parameter   lmpar: find par with |D p| within 10% of delta (par = 0 when the Gauss-Newton step already fits) and the step p
        why          This is the subproblem the trust region poses: the best step for the linear model among the steps no longer than $\Delta$. lmpar, the next section, solves it.
        $$ \min_p \lVert f + J p \rVert \quad \text{subject to} \quad \lVert D p \rVert \le \Delta $$  [hold "the step is at most 10% longer than the trust radius"]
        how          If the Gauss-Newton step is inside the region it is the answer. Otherwise the answer is on the boundary, and solves the damped normal equations below for the one $\lambda > 0$ that makes $\lVert D p \rVert = \Delta$.
        $$ (J^\top J + \lambda D^\top D)\, p = -J^\top f $$  [test "with pivoting and scaling, the step lmpar returns solves (J^T J + par D^T D) x = J^T f to rounding for every radius tried"]
        run          On the first trial $\Delta$ = {delta_in} and lmpar returns $\lambda$ = {par_lm}; on the second, $\Delta$ = {delta_in#2} and $\lambda$ = {par_lm#2}.
      trial          flip the sign (lmpar solves for -p), form x + p and pnorm = |D p|; on the first pass, shrink delta to pnorm
        why          On the first pass $\Delta$ was only a guess; setting it to the length of the step lmpar proposed makes the radius update after it meaningful.
        run          The first trial step has $\lVert D p \rVert$ = {pnorm}, so $\Delta$ becomes {delta}.
      evaluate       evaluate f at x + p (fcn with iflag 1) and count it in nfev
        why          This is the only way to know whether the linear model was right: evaluate the real function at the trial point.
      reductions     compare what the step did with what the linear model said it would do
        actual         ared = 1 - (fnorm1/fnorm)^2, or -1 if the norm grew tenfold or more (1 / LM_RISE_LIMIT; so the square cannot overflow)
          $$ \mathrm{ared} = 1 - \left( \frac{\lVert f(x + p) \rVert}{\lVert f(x) \rVert} \right)^2 $$
          predict        The first trial is the full Gauss-Newton step, the best step there is for the linear model. Will the real sum of squares go down?
          run            No: $\lVert f(x + p) \rVert$ = {fnorm1} against $\lVert f \rVert$ = {fnorm}. The norm grew almost tenfold, so $\mathrm{ared}$ = {actred}.
        predicted      prered = (|J p|^2 + 2 par |D p|^2) / |f|^2, from R, since J^T(f + J p) = -par D^T D p; dirder = the slope of the sum of squares along p, relative
          why            The model's prediction costs no evaluation of $f$: $\lVert J p \rVert = \lVert R P^\top p \rVert$ comes from the factorization.
          $$ \mathrm{pred} = 1 - \frac{\lVert f + J p \rVert^2}{\lVert f \rVert^2} = \frac{\lVert J p \rVert^2 + 2 \lambda \lVert D p \rVert^2}{\lVert f \rVert^2} = t_1^2 + 2 t_2^2 $$  [hold "the predicted reduction is a sum of squares"]
          how            The second form follows from the damped normal equations, $J^\top (f + J p) = -\lambda D^\top D p$. It is a sum of squares, so it is never negative; that is what the hold checks, on every trial.
          run            The linear model predicted $\mathrm{pred}$ = {prered}: with $\lambda = 0$ it promises to remove the whole sum of squares.
        quotient       ratio = ared / prered, or 0 if the model predicts no reduction at all
          $$ \rho = \frac{\mathrm{ared}}{\mathrm{pred}} $$  [hold "a step the test will accept lowers the residual norm"]
          why            $\rho$ is the one number the rest of the pass uses. Near 1, the model can be trusted farther; small or negative, it cannot be trusted this far.
          predict        The model promised to remove the whole sum of squares, and the sum grew almost a hundredfold. What sign will $\rho$ have, and will lmder keep this step?
          run            On the first trial $\rho$ = {ratio}: negative, far below the $10^{-4}$ a step needs to be kept, so $x$ stays where it is. On the second, $\rho$ = {ratio#2}.
      radius         adjust delta (and par with it) by how good the model was
        predict        After a first trial with $\rho$ below zero, will the radius shrink or grow, and by how much?
        run            It shrinks by the most the rule allows: $t$ = {temp}, and $\Delta$ goes from {pnorm} to {delta}.
        $$ \Delta_{\mathrm{new}} = \begin{cases} t \min(\Delta, 10 \lVert D p \rVert),\ t \in [\tfrac{1}{10}, \tfrac12] & \rho \le \tfrac14 \\ 2 \lVert D p \rVert & \rho \ge \tfrac34 \text{ or } \lambda = 0 \\ \Delta & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} $$
        why            A trust region is a claim about how far the model can be trusted, and each trial tests the claim. The rule shrinks the region quickly when the model was badly wrong and grows it when the model was right, so the radius follows the distance over which $f$ is close to linear.
        if the model was poor (ratio <= LM_RATIO_SHRINK, 1/4)
          shrink         new delta = t * min(delta, 10 pnorm), t = 1/2, or where the parabola through the observed values bottoms out if f grew, kept in [LM_SHRINK_MIN, 1/2] = [0.1, 1/2]; par grows by 1/t
            $$ \Delta \le \tfrac12 \Delta_{\mathrm{in}}, \qquad \Delta \le 5 \lVert D p \rVert $$  [hold "a poor step at least halves the radius, and leaves it at most 5 times the step's length"]
            how            When the sum of squares went up, $t$ is where the parabola through $\lVert f \rVert^2$ at $x$, its slope along $p$, and $\lVert f(x + p) \rVert^2$ has its minimum, as a fraction of the step, kept between $\tfrac1{10}$ and $\tfrac12$. $\lambda$ grows by $1/t$, so lmpar starts the next trial near the right $\lambda$.
            run            After the first trial $t$ = {temp}, and $\Delta$ drops from {pnorm} to {delta}.
        else if the model was good (ratio >= LM_RATIO_EXPAND, 3/4), or the step was Gauss-Newton (par = 0)
          expand         delta = 2 pnorm and par halves: the next step may be twice as long as this one
            $$ \Delta = 2 \lVert D p \rVert, \qquad \lambda = \tfrac12 \lambda_{\mathrm{lm}} $$  [hold "a good step, or a Gauss-Newton step, sets the radius to twice the step's length and halves par"]
            run            The first expansion comes on the second trial: $\rho$ = {ratio}, the step was {pnorm} long, and $\Delta$ becomes {delta}.
      if the step reduced the sum of squares by at least LM_RATIO_ACCEPT = 1e-4 of the prediction
        accept         move to x + p: take its residuals, fnorm and |D x|, and count the iteration
          why            Even a step the model predicted poorly is kept if it lowered the sum of squares at all; the poor prediction shows only in the smaller radius.
          predict        The second trial, with $\lambda > 0$ and a region a tenth as wide as the first step, has $\rho$ = {ratio}. Will lmder keep it?
          run            Yes: $\rho$ is far above $10^{-4}$, so the step is taken. The first accepted step moves $x$ to {x}, with $\lVert f \rVert$ = {fnorm}.
      history        write this trial's numbers into a row of hist
      converged      info 1: actual and predicted relative reductions both <= ftol; info 2: delta <= xtol |D x|; info 3: both
        $$ \text{info 1: } |\mathrm{ared}| \le \mathrm{ftol},\ \mathrm{pred} \le \mathrm{ftol},\ \rho \le 2 \qquad \text{info 2: } \Delta \le \mathrm{xtol}\, \lVert D x \rVert $$
        why            Info 1 says no step can lower the sum of squares by more than a relative ftol; info 2 says the region is so small that $x$ is known to a relative xtol.
      stringent      info 5: maxfev evaluations used; 6, 7, 8: ftol, xtol or gtol below what double precision can resolve
  finish         a negative iflag becomes info; with nprint > 0, one last call of fcn with iflag 0; nfev and njev go into counts
    run            The run evaluated $f$ {nfev} times and $J$ {njev} times, and stops at $x$ = {x}.
  return info

lmder1_maxfev  @d17f81f9
  what  The number of evaluations of f lmder1 allows for n variables,
        `100 (n + 1)` as lmder1.f sets maxfev. Every trial step is one
        evaluation after the first, so a hist with this many rows holds
        every trial step lmder1 can take.
  n     $n$  the number of variables

lmder1  @05b73759
  what  The easy-to-use driver: lmder with MINPACK's default settings for a
        single tolerance tol. Returns info, with `info = 8` reported as `4`.
  how   ftol = xtol = tol, gtol = `0`, maxfev = `lmder1_maxfev(n)` =
        `LMDER1_MAXFEV_PER (n + 1)` = `100 (n + 1)`, scaling by the
        Jacobian's column norms (`mode = 1`), factor `LMDER1_FACTOR` =
        `100`, no printing. MINPACK's test driver uses tol =
        $\sqrt{\varepsilon}$, about `1.5e-8`.
  why   The netlib test driver calls this routine, so it is what the Fortran
        oracle runs and what lib/minpack/mgh_check.viv compares.
  n       $n$  the number of parameters
  m       $m$  the number of residuals
  x       $x$  the starting point on entry, the answer on return
  fvec    $f$  the residuals at the answer, on return
  fcn     the user's def, passed on to lmder
  data    the array passed on to fcn
  fjac    $J$  space for the Jacobian; on return its top holds $R$
  tol     the one tolerance lmder1 uses as both ftol and xtol
  ipvt    $\pi$  the column order of the last QR factorization, on return
  counts  on return, the number of evaluations of f and of J
  hist    one row per trial step, passed on to lmder
  info    why lmder stopped, with 8 reported as 4
  diag    $D$  the scaling lmder chooses (mode 1), on return its final value
  qtf     $Q^\top f$  space for lmder's qtf
  maxfev  the most evaluations of f allowed, from lmder1_maxfev
